    "top_frame": "Late in a game that kept slipping away, they recaptured it.",
    "what_this_means": "Seven runs on 11 hits is not chaos. It is efficient execution. They built the lead early, lost it in the fifth when Colorado scored three, then answered with three of their own in the sixth. Zack Gelof supplied the power, delivering two runs with a home run. Alika Williams went 3-for-3 and kept the line moving. The real test came in the aftermath: the bullpen held. Suarez, Barlow, Leiter, and Alvarado combined for 4.2 innings without a mistake, each inning tighter than the last. This was not a scramble. This was a team that knew what it needed and executed it when it mattered.",
    "what_to_watch": "Four straight wins. One game back in the division. The margin is built on nights like this. Balanced production, early control, and a bullpen that doesn't panic when the lead gets tight. They had runners in scoring position 15 times and converted just three of them, which sounds like waste until you remember they won by two. That is the tension this team lives in: broad opportunities but narrow conversion. They are learning to accept three converted chances and a clean finish over a single dominant inning. The pitching gave them the frame. The bats filled it just enough. That math holds exactly until it doesn't.",
